node_modules/
.next/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

# `next-env.d.ts` is NOT ignored, though Next's own default template ignores it.
# Next regenerates it, but it is a source file: it holds the triple-slash references that
# make `next/image`, `next/link` and the CSS-module types resolve. A clone without it
# typechecks differently from one with it, and "the file is regenerated anyway" is only true
# after a successful `next dev` or `next build` -- which is exactly what a fresh clone is
# trying to do. Committed on purpose.

# Environment overrides for the two upstream services. The defaults live in
# next.config.ts so a fresh clone runs against the standard local ports; this file is
# only for pointing the console somewhere else.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Pages saved by `tools/capture-scenarios.mjs` while it produces the reports in
# docs/evidence/. They are the INPUT to a report, and they are kept only when a run fails so
# that the failure can be inspected -- the committed evidence is the report. Ignored rather
# than committed because a saved page is a build artifact: it embeds the build id and the
# upstream values of the moment it was taken, and a stale one in the repository would be read
# as documentation of current behaviour.
.captures/

# The runtime config, generated by `scripts/build-runtime-config.mjs` from a deployment
# record. Ignored because it IS a copy of that record's addresses: committed, it would be a
# second place for the address to be wrong, and the wrong one would be the one nobody updated.
# The script is the thing to read. Regenerate with:
#   node scripts/build-runtime-config.mjs
public/api/config

# The captured index snapshot, written by `scripts/capture-index-snapshot.mjs` and published by
# the Pages workflow. Ignored for a sharper version of the reason above: these files ARE the
# figures the site shows, frozen at the moment of one build. Committed, they would be a second
# copy of the index that nothing updates and nothing ages -- the page reads its own `updatedAt`
# and `staleSeconds` from whichever copy it was served, so a stale committed one would look
# exactly like a fresh one while being months old. That is the specific failure this project
# keeps writing down, and the fix is that the snapshot only ever exists as a build artifact.
#
# Capture locally with the service running:
#   node scripts/capture-index-snapshot.mjs --service http://127.0.0.1:8787 --out public/api
#
# ALL FIVE, and `candles` was missing from this list until it was added here: the path was added
# to the capture, the workflow and the README when the chart was brought into the snapshot, and
# this list -- the one place whose whole job is to keep the snapshot out of version control -- was
# not updated with it. `public/api/candles` was therefore the single file of the five that `git
# add` would have taken, which is exactly the case the paragraph above says must not happen.
public/api/status
public/api/summary
public/api/events
public/api/price
public/api/candles

# The static export, and the staging directory the Pages workflow builds it in.
out/
_site/
